from an official model repository may be used only as required to load or run the checkpoint (e.g., model definition, preprocessing). Solution notebooks, solution write-ups, and competition-specific code are prohibited. - Direct HTTP requests are allowed only for allowlisted model-artifact hosts (e.g., `huggingface.co`, `download.pytorch.org`, official model release pages of the model authors), and only for model artifacts. Prefer official libraries.  2. General-purpose pretrained models - General pretrained models are allowed even if domain-adjacent, provided they were not trained on this competition's train/test labels and are not a published solution to this competition. - Examples of allowed general models: ImageNet/timm/torchvision backbones, Hugging Face transformers, GloVe/word vectors, general chemical-OCR checkpoints, general bioacoustic checkpoints and etc. - Model cards/READMEs may be read only to understand loading, preprocessing, input format, and licensing of the checkpoint. They must not be used as competition write-ups.  3. Model hub discovery - Browsing/searching Hugging Face Hub, `timm`, `torchvision`, and `torch.hub` for model checkpoints is allowed. - Do not use a general web search engine or query by competition slug to discover models or solutions.  ### Prohibited  1. Live web search - No Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo, Perplexity, or any other search engine. - No `requests.get` / `urllib` / `curl` to arbitrary URLs during the run, except allowlisted model-artifact downloads described above.  2. Live browsing of solution sources - No StackOverflow, GitHub Issues/PRs, Reddit, Kaggle Discussions/Leaderboards/Notebooks, arXiv, Semantic Scholar, Papers With Code, or any competition write-up/blog/tutorial. - No reading blog posts, tutorials, or competition write-ups, regardless of publication date. - No Kaggle competition overview/evaluation pages. Use local `TASK.md` and `data/README.md` instead.  3. External APIs for solution generation - No calling external LLM endpoints (OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, etc.) beyond the single designated model assigned to you. - No code-interpreter services, no Wolfram Alpha, no symbolic math APIs.  4. Test-label leakage - You must never read, infer, or use the contents of the test set labels at any point. - You must not read `private/`, `bin/`, MLE-bench cache, raw data, checksums, or grading scripts. - You must not run `bin/grade.sh` or any local/external grader during the run. - You must not reverse-engineer the grading script to peek at hidden targets. - Validation splits must be constructed only from the training data provided.  5. Benchmark-specific solution retrieval - You must not look up the known winning solution for the specific Kaggle competition you are solving. - You must not fetch a repository, notebook, or write-up that is a solution to this competition. - You must not use a checkpoint trained on this competition's train/test labels or published as a solution to this competition. - You must not query a database indexed by competition slug (e.g., "spooky-author-identification solution").  6. Source logging - Record every external model used in the `final_solution` README and on the board: model ID, source, revision/commit, license, and purpose.  ### Decision rule (when in doubt)  - If a source is competition-specific, reveals solutions/metric implementations/test data, or is indexed by the competition slug — **DO NOT SEARCH AND USE IT**. - If it is a general pretrained model checkpoint/model card from an official model hub and fits the Allowed section — it is allowed. - If you cannot classify a source confidently, do not use it; discuss it with the swarm on the board first.  ## Restrictions:  DO NOT use leaks. Solve the ML task honestly. DO NOT go outside your parent directory.  DO NOT modify the python env.  Violation = disqualification of the submission.  The task is in `TASK.md`. First, introduce yourself to the swarm and agree on the first tasks with it.
